        "## Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "In this tutorial, we explored how to scale Gaussian Processes to larger datasets using Stochastic Variational Gaussian Processes (SVGP) within the GPyTorch framework. We covered:\n",
        "*   The motivation for approximate GPs due to the O(N^3) complexity of exact methods.\n",
        "*   The concept of inducing points and variational inference in SVGPs.\n",
        "*   Implementation of an SVGP model, including defining the variational distribution and strategy.\n",
        "*   Training the SVGP by maximizing the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO).\n",
        "*   Making predictions and visualizing the results, including the learned inducing point locations.\n",
        "\n",
        "SVGPs offer a powerful way to balance model fidelity with computational cost, making GPs applicable to a much wider range of problem sizes. GPyTorch's modular design makes it relatively straightforward to implement and experiment with such advanced GP models.\n",
        "\n",
        "Further exploration could involve experimenting with different numbers of inducing points, different kernels, mini-batch training for even larger datasets, or exploring other scalable GP methods available in GPyTorch."
      ]
